er/id #uuid "75bf22e4-a784-5bcd-b8d2-7d5f8f5f790c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A⇒B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bdd0e47-18e5-5297-a187-1fce63926a25" :answer/id #uuid "3bdd0e47-18e5-5297-a187-1fce63926a25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B⇒A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2b5afd8-4be0-5217-be25-bcd0b5c0bdf4" :question/id #uuid "c2b5afd8-4be0-5217-be25-bcd0b5c0bdf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-183490" :question/problem "Find the missing values in the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $A$ is sufficient for $B$ | {{field-1}} |\n| For $B,$ it is sufficient that $A$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ | {{field-3}} |\n| For $B,$ it is necessary that $A$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"$A$ is sufficient for $B$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"For $B,$ it is sufficient that $A$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"$B$ is necessary for $A$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"For $B,$ it is necessary that $A$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $A$ is sufficient for $B$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| For $B,$ it is sufficient that $A$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| For $B,$ it is necessary that $A$ | $B⇒A$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e2f43cd8-654f-514d-b93e-2c9c4d481c4a" "answer-field-9eb88832-ac8c-5502-8207-ce8fa8b6e43d" "a