ield/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ae6d9e6-00c4-5ee5-bc3f-ec52b0c0dc4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c23cefd-456f-5f0f-941c-8d48c26de887" "answer-aae41545-3194-5698-95df-6153f725ed0b" "answer-3ae6d9e6-00c4-5ee5-bc3f-ec52b0c0dc4e" "answer-401381c3-e289-5f7e-b008-96dcdfd5d85d" "answer-54dc99bd-e45d-56d3-8b2c-a9cf11872588"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c23cefd-456f-5f0f-941c-8d48c26de887" :answer/id #uuid "3c23cefd-456f-5f0f-941c-8d48c26de887" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is positive if $x>0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aae41545-3194-5698-95df-6153f725ed0b" :answer/id #uuid "aae41545-3194-5698-95df-6153f725ed0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is positive implies $x>0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ae6d9e6-00c4-5ee5-bc3f-ec52b0c0dc4e" :answer/id #uuid "3ae6d9e6-00c4-5ee5-bc3f-ec52b0c0dc4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is positive is equivalent to $x>0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-401381c3-e289-5f7e-b008-96dcdfd5d85d" :answer/id #uuid "401381c3-e289-5f7e-b008-96dcdfd5d85d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is positive or $x>0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54dc99bd-e45d-56d3-8b2c-a9cf11872588" :answer/id #uuid "54dc99bd-e45d-56d3-8b2c-a9cf11872588" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is positive, provided that $x>0.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198848 :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a grammatical construction for the biconditional $P⇔Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P⇔Q:$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that is NOT a grammatical construction for $P⇔Q$ is \"$P$ is sufficient for $Q$\" (this is the grammatical construction of $P⇒Q).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186196159 :answer-field/correct 17592186196160 :answer-field/choices ["answer-c147d677-3c2a-5885-b758-cf314fe61394"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c147d677-3c2a-5885-b758-cf314fe61394" :answer/id #uuid "c147d677-3c2a-5885-b758-cf314fe61394" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198699 :question/problem "Express $A⇔B$ as a sentence given the following:\n\n- $A:$ $4$ is even.\n\n- $B:$ $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P⇔Q:$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the correct grammatical construction of $A⇔B$ is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $4$ is even **if and only if** $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194820 :answer-field/correct "answer-a00e2dfe-e552-51bc-9a13-9c47db99cf73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48628bed-966b-5e82-a7ec-d2c0f8647a12" "answer-aa30bfac-4d0d-532b-9f89-0d0c454c4300" "answer-a00e2dfe-e552-51bc-9a13-9c47db99cf73" "answer-99a4a2a4-0fa6-55cb-9516-a6da6381f1c4" "answer-37046a87-18c5-507f-bc35-a399578045cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48628bed-966b-5e82-a7ec-d2c0f8647a12" :answer/id #uuid "48628bed-966b-5e82-a7ec-d2c0f8647a12" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ is even and $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa30bfac-4d0d-532b-9f89-0d0c454c4300" :answer/id #uuid "aa30bfac-4d0d-532b-9f89-0d0c454c4300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ is even if $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a00e2dfe-e552-51bc-9a13-9c47db99cf73" :answer/id #uuid "a00e2dfe-e552-51bc-9a13-9c47db99cf73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ is even if and only if $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99a4a2a4-0fa6-55cb-9516-a6da6381f1c4" :answer/id #uuid "99a4a2a4-0fa6-55cb-9516-a6da6381f1c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ is even only if $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37046a87-18c5-507f-bc35-a399578045cc" :answer/id #uuid "37046a87-18c5-507f-bc35-a399578045cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ is even or $4$ is divisible by $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-204c3d5d-7433-5e9d-bacd-9011685dc6a9" :question/id #uuid "204c3d5d-7433-5e9d-bacd-9011685dc6a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-171419" :question/problem "Express $P⇔Q$ as a sentence given the following:\n\n- $P:$ $A$ triangle is right.\n\n- $Q:$ $A$ triangle has two complementary angles." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P⇔Q:$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the correct grammatical construction of $P⇔Q$ is the following:\n\n$\\,$ A triangle is right **if and only if** it has two complementary angles." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-852a81f7-8616-56ae-ae1c-84fc0268a724"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-852a81f7-8616-56ae-ae1c-84fc0268a724" :answer-field/id #uuid "852a81f7-8616-56ae-ae1c-84fc0268a724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5fc85f2a-aa86-597d-9f3c-026f5190527b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58aef142-5159-5aa0-94fe-d7e18c41a235" "answer-c7595d95-071f-5d6e-8261-384302cfad91" "answer-1f5bb8ac-767a-5871-92a9-37dddfee9d64" "answer-5fc85f2a-aa86-597d-9f3c-026f5190527b" "answer-9522521b-1b5e-5d5c-ac86-1c40f78398bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58aef142-5159-5aa0-94fe-d7e18c41a235" :answer/id #uuid "58aef142-5159-5aa0-94fe-d7e18c41a235" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle has two complementary angles only if it is a right triangle." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7595d95-071f-5d6e-8261-384302cfad91" :answer/id #uuid "c7595d95-071f-5d6e-8261-384302cfad91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle has two complementary angles, if it is a right triangle." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f5bb8ac-767a-5871-92a9-37dddfee9d64" :answer/id #uuid "1f5bb8ac-767a-5871-92a9-37dddfee9d64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle is right and it has two complementary angles." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fc85f2a-aa86-597d-9f3c-026f5190527b" :answer/id #uuid "5fc85f2a-aa86-597d-9f3c-026f5190527b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle is right if and only if it has two complementary angles." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9522521b-1b5e-5d5c-ac86-1c40f78398bb" :answer/id #uuid "9522521b-1b5e-5d5c-ac86-1c40f78398bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle is right, provided that it has two complementary angles." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199569 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've seen several grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals. In this lesson, we'll review all those that are commonly used.\n\nThe implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ has the following grammatical constructions:\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nFor example, \n\n$\\qquad$ **If** $4 \\mid n,$ **then** $2 \\mid n$\n\nis equivalent to the following:\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **implies** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **only if** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $2 \\mid n,$ **if** $4 \\mid n$\n\nUsing these grammatical constructions correctly within a particular mathematical context can be tricky. It's important to go through each and understand why it's equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ So, let's do that. We assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q{:}$ If $P$ is true, then $Q$ *must* be true.\n\n- $P$ only if $Q{:}$ The *only* way that $P$ is true is if $Q$ is also true.\n\n- $Q$ if $P{:}$ $Q$ is *guaranteed* to be true if $P$ is true.\n\n- $P$ implies $Q.$ This is the same as \"If $P,$ then $Q.$\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199593 :tutorial/content "Recall that, in terms of necessary and sufficient conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{Sufficient}\\phantom{|}}_{P} \\Rightarrow \\underbrace{\\textrm{Necessary}}_{Q}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following equivalent grammatical constructions:\n\n- $P$ is **sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- For $Q,$ it is **sufficient** that $P$\n\n- $Q$ is **necessary** for $P$\n\n- For $P,$ it is **necessary** that $Q$\n\nLet's go back to the following true implication that we encountered in a previous lesson:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nWe can use the following grammatical constructions in terms of sufficient conditions:\n\n- $a > 0$ is a *sufficient condition* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- $a > 0$ is *sufficient* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- For $a^2 > 0,$ it is *sufficient* that $a > 0$\n\nThese all mean the same thing, namely that $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0.$\n\nFor necessary conditions, we have:\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary condition* for $a > 0$\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is *necessary* for $a > 0$\n\n- For $a > 0,$ it is *necessary* that $a^2 > 0$\n\nThese all mean that if $a > 0$ it follows that $a^2 > 0,$ but $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199594 :tutorial/content "Implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ also can be written using \"whenever\" and \"provided\" as follows:  \n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P$\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P$\n\nLet's again go through each one and convince ourselves that they're all equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ Again, we assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q{:}$ Whenever $P$ is true, $Q$ is guaranteed to be true.\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true whenever $P$ is true.\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true provided that $P$ is true.\n\nLet's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199595 :tutorial/content "The grammatical constructions for biconditionals $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ are slightly more straightforward than for conditionals.\n\nWe've already seen the following constructions:\n\n- $P$ **if and only if** $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **necessary and sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **equivalent** to $Q$\n\nThe following constructions are also commonly used:\n\n- $P$ **iff** $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ **and conversely**\n\nHere, \"iff\" is simply shorthand for \"if and only if.\"\n\nThe construction \"If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\" is interesting. Recall that the converse of $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is $Q\\Rightarrow P.$ If $P\\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Rightarrow P$ are both true, we have that $P$ implies $Q$ and $Q$ implies $P,$ i.e., $P\\Leftrightarrow Q.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198539 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
